<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khoja Niyaz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1889–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Khoja Niyaz Hajji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khoja Niyaz was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> leader from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a> (Hami) who headed the Kumul rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> provincial government from 1931. In 1933 he was named president of the First East Turkestan Republic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, although he never governed from there. He later came to terms with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheng-shicai">Sheng Shicai</a> and served in the provincial government, but was arrested and executed in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khoja-niyaz.jpg" alt="Khoja Niyaz" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoja-Niyaz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khoja Niyaz was born in a small mountain village in Kumul Prefecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. At eighteen he joined a 1907 uprising of peasants and mountaineers against Shah Maqsud, the hereditary ruler of Kumul under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>. After its defeat he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> region, studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astana">Astana</a> religious school, where he came to know the brothers Maksut and Mahmut Muhiti, and then made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, taking the title Hajji.</p><p>In 1912 he joined another rising against the Kumul Khanate, led by Temur Halpa, as an adviser. After Governor Yang Zengxin had Temur Halpa killed at a banquet on 6 September 1913, he fled again. In 1916 he reached Jarkent in Russian Semiryechye, where after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> he helped organise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> self-defence groups and met the revolutionary Abdulla Rozibakiev.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in 1923 and organised underground groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yining">Ghulja</a> and Urumchi. After the death of Shah Mahsut in March 1930 he became adviser to the new ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a>, but Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-shuren">Jin Shuren</a> abolished the khanate and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> there. This sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumul-rebellion">Kumul Rebellion</a> of 1931, in which the Hui warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-zhongying">Ma Zhongying</a> joined Khoja Niyaz and Yulbars Khan; the Mongolian People's Republic also sent him winter clothing, tents and 120 rifles.</p><p>By February 1933 the provincial government held no more than 10% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-zhongying">Ma Zhongying</a> seized the arsenal of Jimsar on 28 May 1933, Khoja Niyaz turned to General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheng-shicai">Sheng Shicai</a>, signing an alliance on 4 June 1933 that recognised his claim to the lands south of the Tengri Tagh, and the Soviets armed him. Defeated by Tungan troops at the Battle of Toksun, he took the presidency of the Turkic Islamic Republic of East Turkestan, which broke that agreement. The republic fell to Tungan forces and Soviet intervention by June 1934.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheng-shicai">Sheng Shicai</a> then made him Vice-Chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> government, but he was kept apart from his troops in Urumchi under the watch of Soviet agents, and his request to meet Stalin was refused. After the rebellion of the 6th Uyghur Division under Mahmut Muhiti in 1937 he was arrested in November of that year. He and 120 followers were labelled counter-revolutionaries and Japanese agents, charges he called fabrications of Sheng Shicai, and he was sentenced to death with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s approval. His last words were reported as: &quot;I will die, but my People will continue to live.&quot;</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khoja Niyaz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic was based on Islamic law and Turkic nationalism, with Sabit Damulla as prime minister and the Kumul rebel leader <b>Khoja Niyaz</b> named president, though he never ruled from Kashgar.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-east-turkestan-republic">First East Turkestan Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hami Zhiliting. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urumqi">Ürümqi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.82,87.61&amp;year=1922" aria-label="Ürümqi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6653 611 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Khoja Niyaz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7103 881h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7103 881h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.82,87.61&amp;year=1922">What was here in 1922? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
